SUMMARY:Applied Micro – Naila Shofia (National University Singapore) – 
 You're Fired\, My Lord: Experimental Evidence on Democratic Narratives and
  Accountability
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Naila Shofia: “You're Fired\, My Lord: Experimental Evidence 
 on Democratic Narratives and Accountability”\nhttps://sites.google.com/v
 iew/nailashofia-com/home\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/89372815664?pw
 d=hMmKuMrbVw8XIryww2pen75K6o3XaY.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps
 .app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe study how lay conceptions o
 f democracy shape voter preferences and behavior using a large\, multi-wav
 e survey experiment conducted during Indonesia’s 2024 general election. 
 Using random utility models and a discrete choice experiment\, we show tha
 t voters with different conceptions of democracy place systematically diff
 erent weights on candidate characteristics. To provide causal evidence\, w
 e randomly assign an animated video treatment framing elections as instrum
 ents of accountability\, where politicians are "employees" of the voter. T
 he intervention significantly increases the weight voters assign to track 
 record and policy alignment in candidate evaluation. Crucially\, the treat
 ment also affects voting behavior in the real-stakes presidential election
  by reducing last-minute switching to the poll-predicted winner. These eff
 ects are driven primarily by voters with limited prior understanding of el
 ectoral accountability. Extensive analyses support the interpretation that
  the intervention changes the lay understanding of democratic representati
 on among those who did not initially think in terms of political agency. T
 he results suggest that narrative-focused interventions aimed at "soft fea
 tures" of democracy may offer a promising path to enhance democratic accou
 ntability. 
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/89372815664?pwd=hMmKuMrbVw8XIryww2pen75K6o3Xa
 Y.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
