DESCRIPTION:Romain Gauriot: “Temperature and Leniency: Man vs Machine”\
 nhttps://sites.google.com/site/romaingauriot/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoo
 m.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoog
 le Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe use ov
 er 7 million speeding fines from Queensland\, Australia to study how outdo
 or temperature affects police officer discretion. Comparing officer-issued
  fines to those issued by automated speed cameras\, which detect violation
 s without exercising discretion\, isolates the effect of temperature on of
 ficer decisions from temperature-driven changes in driver behaviour. Offic
 ers are 3.5 times more likely than mobile cameras to issue a fine 1 km/h b
 elow a penalty threshold\, indicating widespread leniency. The share of le
 nient fines is lower on hotter days\, but this is driven by changes in pat
 rol location. On hot days\, enforcement shifts toward areas with lower bas
 eline leniency. Conditional on location\, temperature has no effect on off
 icer discretion.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:88c6e155b480913b964acd8235099254@generated
DTSTAMP:20260728T201026Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260728T201026Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260806T110000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260806T121500
SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – Jay Lu (Joint with ET) (UCLA) – Crea
 tive Ownership in the Age of AI
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Jay Lu (Joint with ET): “Creative Ownership in the Age of AI
 ”\nhttp://www.econ.ucla.edu/jay/about.html\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom
 .us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogl
 e Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nCopyright 
 law asks whether a new work is “substantially similar” to an existing 
 one\, but generative AI can closely imitate a creator’s style without co
 pying any content—a capability now central to ongoing litigation. We arg
 ue that existing definitions of creative ownership are ill-suited to this 
 setting and propose a new criterion: an AI-generated output depends essent
 ially on an existing work if it could not have been generated without that
  work in the training corpus\, and it is permissible if it does not depend
  essentially on any existing work. We establish structural properties of t
 he set of permissible outputs and prove a sharp asymptotic dichotomy: when
  the creative process is light-tailed\, dependence on individual works eve
 ntually vanishes\, so in the long run the criterion places no limits on AI
  generation\; on the other hand\, when it is heavy-tailed\, individual wor
 ks remain pivotal and the criterion continues to bind even for arbitrarily
  large corpora.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
