SUMMARY:Macroeconomics – Yasin Kursat Onder (Ghent) – Debt Relief Witho
 ut Public Spending: Corporate Debt Moratoria
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Yasin Kursat Onder: “Debt Relief Without Public Spending: Cor
 porate Debt Moratoria”\nhttps://sites.google.com/view/yasin-kursat-onder
 \n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84055677057?pwd=q1KyfcUkOWfMfEThCYsc9f
 s2jh2vCa.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9p
 SbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nCan crisis-time debt relief stimulate economic activit
 y without additional public spending? We study corporate debt moratoria—
 temporary suspensions of scheduled loan payments—using Colombian adminis
 trative credit-registry data. Exploiting a sharp regulatory eligibility th
 reshold\, we show that financially distressed firms granted a moratorium o
 btain new credit more often and on better terms: new loans are larger\, sp
 reads are lower\, and subsequent delinquency declines. These credit-market
  improvements translate into real effects: among stressed firms near the e
 ligibility cutoff\, temporary payment suspensions raise annual investment 
 by about 16 percent and increase employment. We then use these reduced-for
 m responses to discipline a quantitative model of corporate borrowing\, de
 fault\, and investment. The model shows that one-off moratoria can provide
  valuable liquidity insurance\, but that recurrent moratoria are priced ex
  ante and can reduce welfare through equilibrium pricing and portfolio sub
 stitution. Contract design is central: relief that includes forgiveness of
  accrued interest generates larger welfare gains than pure payment deferra
 l.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84055677057?pwd=q1KyfcUkOWfMfEThCYsc9fs2jh2vC
 a.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
